  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "Let \\(f\\) be a polynomial function such that, for all real \\(x,\\) \\[ f(x^2+1)=x^4+5x^2+3. \\] For all real \\(x,\\) \\(f(x^2-1)\\) is",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(x^4+5x^2+1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(x^4+x^2-3\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(x^4-5x^2+1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(x^4+x^2+3\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "none of these"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1983-p18.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "With \\(t=x^2+1,\\) \\[ \\begin{aligned} x^4+5x^2+3 &=(t-1)^2\\\\ &\\quad+5(t-1)+3\\\\ &=t^2+3t-1. \\end{aligned} \\] Hence \\(f(t)=t^2+3t-1.\\) Substituting \\(t=x^2-1\\) gives \\[ \\begin{aligned} f(x^2-1) &=(x^2-1)^2\\\\ &\\quad+3(x^2-1)-1\\\\ &=x^4+x^2-3. \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Rewrite the right side as a polynomial in \\(x^2+1\\)",
        "Setting \\(t=x^2+1\\) gives \\(f(t)=t^2+3t-1\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
